Let's consider these functions:

Function LISP-IMPLEMENTATION-TYPE, LISP-IMPLEMENTATION-VERSION

Function SHORT-SITE-NAME, LONG-SITE-NAME

Function MACHINE-INSTANCE

Function MACHINE-TYPE

Function MACHINE-VERSION

Function SOFTWARE-TYPE, SOFTWARE-VERSION

Here is their results on sbcl on macOS:

    (pprint
     (loop for fun in '(LISP-IMPLEMENTATION-TYPE LISP-IMPLEMENTATION-VERSION
                        SHORT-SITE-NAME LONG-SITE-NAME
                        MACHINE-INSTANCE
                        MACHINE-TYPE
                        MACHINE-VERSION
                        SOFTWARE-TYPE SOFTWARE-VERSION)
           collect (list fun (funcall fun))))
    --> 
    ((lisp-implementation-type "SBCL")
     (lisp-implementation-version "2.6.3")
     (short-site-name "thalassa")
     (long-site-name "thalassa.informatimago.com")
     (machine-instance "thalassa")
     (machine-type "ARM64")
     (machine-version "Apple M3 Pro")
     (software-type "Darwin")
     (software-version "25.5.0"))


 Note: most of these values are determined at run-time. (of course,
 lisp-implementation-type and version is static.  But a binary
 compiled on a given
 OS-name/OS-version/processor/processor-version/hostname could execute
 on a different but compatible combination. It's the run-time
 combination that must be returned by these functions.
 
 
 | Function                    | SBCL on macOS                | bocl on…          | bocl on... |
 |-----------------------------+------------------------------+-------------------+------------|
 | lisp-implementation-type    | "SBCL"                       | "bocl"            | "bocl"     |
 | lisp-implementation-version | "2.6.3"                      | "1.0.0"           | "1.0.0"    |
 | short-site-name             | "thalassa"                   | hostname          | …          |
 | long-site-name              | "thalassa.informatimago.com" | fqdn              | …          |
 | machine-instance            | "thalassa"                   | hostname          | …          |
 | machine-type                | "ARM64"                      | processor-name    | …          |
 | machine-version             | "Apple M3 Pro"               | processor-version | …          |
 | software-type               | "Darwin"                     | os-name           | …          |
 | software-version            | "25.5.0"                     | os-version        | …          |




Since we load a prelude which is an important part of the
implementation, but is also distributed as source-form and could be
modified, independently, let the prelude file define its own version:

bocl:*prelude-version*

and if it's different than bocl binary version, then
lisp-implementation-version returns a concatenation:
(format nil "~A-~A" bocl:*version* bocl:*prelude-version*)



The results of these functions:

- short-site-name 
- long-site-name  
- machine-instance

may be overriden by the user by setting these variables:
 
- bocl:*short-site-name*
- bocl:*long-site-name*
- bocl:*machine-instance*

If they're nil or unbound, then the implementation will try to obtain them from the OS. (hostname)


